    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking into some premature longitudinal cracking we're seeing on a new asphalt section here in Phoenix. Can you give me a breakdown of the primary factors that typically lead to this kind of pavement failure, specifically looking at the roles of thermal expansion, bitumen viscosity, and subgrade compaction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking on asphalt sections is often caused by a combination of factors, including thermal expansion and traffic loads. Thermal expansion plays a key role as it can cause the asphalt pavement to contract and expand with temperature changes, leading to stress and subsequent cracking. Bitumen viscosity also impacts cracking, as lower viscosities can lead to greater susceptibility to cracking due to increased flow stress. Poor subgrade compaction can exacerbate this issue by failing to distribute loads effectively and create significant settling or rutting, which can break the bond between the asphalt layer and the underlying subgrade, leading to further cracking and damage.",
      "source": "target",
